## The current position

**No individually named practitioner has yet signed off the monograph set.** We are not going
to invent one, and we are not going to use a vague phrase like "reviewed by our Ayurvedic
team" to imply something that has not happened.

What has been done:

- Every monograph derives from sourced reference material rather than from generated prose,
  and the sources are listed on each page.
- Pharmacopoeial and regulatory status fields come from the named pharmacopoeias, not from
  secondary summaries.
- The <a href={rel('/shirodhara/')}>Shirodhara guide</a> pages are written from classical
  sources and from practice knowledge held inside Age Ayurveda, and they distinguish
  explicitly between what the Samhitas contain and what is later Kerala tradition.
- Mechanical checks run on every build: no page may state the contested classical herb count
  for Chyawanprash, no disease term may appear inside a product block, and restricted and
  metallic preparations are excluded from publication entirely.

What has not been done:

- Line-by-line review of each monograph by a qualified vaidya.
- Independent verification of every research summary against the original paper.

## Why this page exists

Search engines and answer engines both weight named, credentialled review, and it would be
easy to write a page that gestures at expertise without committing to any. That page would be
a lie, and a reference work that lies about its own provenance is worth nothing.

When a named practitioner has reviewed a section, this page will say who, which section, and
when. Until then it says this.

## If you are qualified and want to help

We are looking for a qualified Ayurvedic physician willing to be credited by name for
reviewing sections of the Nighantu. Corrections from anyone, credentialled or not, are welcome
at <a href={rel('/corrections/')}>corrections</a>.
</Page>
